/* Countdown quiz deck: chem. Generated from the revision notes — do not edit by hand. */
QZ.load("chem", {"chapters":{"1":["1","States of matter"],"2":["2","Atomic structure"],"3":["3","Chemical bonding"],"4":["4","Chemical formulae and equations"],"5":["5","Chemical calculations"],"6":["6","Electrochemistry"],"7":["7","Chemical energetics"],"8":["8","Rates of reaction"],"9":["9","Reversible reactions and equilibrium"],"10":["10","Redox reactions"],"11":["11","Acids and bases"],"12":["12","Preparation of salts"],"13":["13","The Periodic Table"],"14":["14","Metallic elements and alloys"],"15":["15","Reactivity of metals"],"16":["16","Extraction and corrosion of metals"],"17":["17","Chemistry of our environment"],"18":["18","Introduction to organic chemistry"],"19":["19","Reactions of organic compounds"],"20":["20","Petrochemicals and polymers"],"21":["21","Experimental design and separation techniques"],"22":["22","Chemical analysis"]},"topics":{"chem-form":["Formulae & calculations","Write the formula"],"chem-react":["Reactions & equations","Give the reactions and equations"],"chem-law":["Laws, theories & rules","State it"],"chem-test":["Tests (notes)","Describe the test and result"],"chem-uses":["Uses","Give the uses"],"chem-facts":["Short facts","Answer"]},"cards":[["n-c1-law-kinetic-particle-theory-of","chem-law",1,"Kinetic particle theory of matter","<ol>\n<li>All matter is made of tiny <strong>particles</strong> (atoms, molecules or ions). 2. The particles are <strong>always moving</strong>. 3. The <strong>higher the temperature, the faster</strong> they move (more kinetic energy). 4. There are <strong>forces of attraction</strong> between particles. 5. Heavier particles move more slowly than lighter ones at the same temperature.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Explaining changes of state</strong> <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>: on <strong>heating</strong>, particles gain kinetic energy; in a solid they <strong>vibrate more</strong> until they overcome the forces holding them in fixed positions → <strong>melting</strong>. In a liquid, particles move faster until they have enough energy to <strong>overcome the attractive forces</strong> and escape as gas → <strong>boiling</strong>. On <strong>cooling</strong> the reverse happens: particles lose energy, forces pull them together → <strong>condensing</strong>, <strong>freezing</strong>.</p>",1,""],["n-c1-fac-pure-substances-have-a-sha","chem-facts",1,"How do melting and boiling points show purity?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Pure substances have a sharp m.p. and b.p.; mixtures melt and boil over a range.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Used to test purity.</p></div>",0,""],["n-c1-fac-state-symbols-s-solid-l-li","chem-facts",1,"The four state symbols?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">State symbols: (s) solid, (l) liquid, (g) gas, (aq) dissolved in water.</p>",0,""],["n-c2-for-proton-number","chem-form",2,"Proton number","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">Z = number of protons</div><p class=\"qn-fn\">Also equals the number of electrons in a neutral atom (atoms have no overall charge).</p>",0,""],["n-c2-for-mass-number","chem-form",2,"Mass number","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">A = number of protons + number of neutrons</div>",0,""],["n-c2-for-electrons-in-an-atom","chem-form",2,"Electrons in an atom","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">number of electrons = Z</div><p class=\"qn-fn\">For an atom only. For an ion: electrons = Z − charge. e.g. Na⁺: 11 − 1 = 10 electrons; Cl⁻: 17 + 1 = 18 electrons.</p>",0,""],["n-c2-for-number-of-neutrons","chem-form",2,"Number of neutrons","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">neutrons = A − Z</div>",0,""],["n-c2-for-the-atomic-mass-standard","chem-form",2,"The atomic mass standard","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">1 atomic mass unit = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">1</span><span class=\"de\">12</span></span> × mass of one atom of carbon-12</div><p class=\"qn-fn\">This is why Ar is defined \"compared with 1/12 of the mass of an atom of ¹²C\".</p>",0,""],["n-c2-for-relative-atomic-mass-from","chem-form",2,"Relative atomic mass from isotopes","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">Ar = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">(mass₁ × % abundance₁) + (mass₂ × % abundance₂)</span><span class=\"de\">100</span></span></div><p class=\"qn-fn\">e.g. chlorine: 75% ³⁵Cl and 25% ³⁷Cl → Ar = (35 × 75 + 37 × 25) ÷ 100 = 35.5.</p>",1,""],["n-c2-for-atomic-symbols","chem-form",2,"Atomic symbols","<div class=\"qn-fbox\"><span class=\"nuc\"><span class=\"ns\"><span>A</span><span>Z</span></span>X</span></div><p class=\"qn-fn\">Mass number A on top, proton number Z at the bottom. e.g. <span class=\"nuc\"><span class=\"ns\"><span>12</span><span>6</span></span>C</span> has 6 p, 6 e, 6 n; <span class=\"nuc\"><span class=\"ns\"><span>35</span><span>17</span></span>Cl</span>⁻ has 17 p, 18 e, 18 n.</p>",0,""],["n-c2-law-electrons-in-shells-bohr-s","chem-law",2,"Electrons in shells (Bohr's model)","<p>What you need is the <strong>shell model</strong>, not the history: <strong>electrons are arranged in shells (energy levels) around the nucleus</strong>, filling the innermost shell first (2, 8, 8…). <strong>Group VIII noble gases have a full outer shell</strong>; <strong>the number of outer-shell electrons equals the group number in Groups I to VII</strong>; <strong>the number of occupied shells equals the period number</strong>.</p>",0,""],["n-c2-fac-almost-all-the-mass-of-an","chem-facts",2,"Where is almost all the mass of an atom?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Almost all the mass of an atom is in the nucleus.</p>",0,""],["n-c2-fac-atoms-are-neutral-number-o","chem-facts",2,"Why are atoms neutral?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Atoms are neutral: number of protons = number of electrons.</p>",0,""],["n-c3-fac-group-i-ions-are-1-group-i","chem-facts",3,"Charges of the ions in Groups I, II, VI and VII?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Group I ions are 1+, Group II 2+, Group VI 2−, Group VII 1−.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Charge = electrons lost (metals) or gained (non-metals).</p></div>",0,""],["n-c3-fac-ionic-compounds-conduct-wh","chem-facts",3,"When do ionic compounds conduct electricity?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Ionic compounds conduct when molten or dissolved, never as solids.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Exam favourite.</p></div>",0,""],["n-c4-law-charges-of-common-ions","chem-law",4,"Charges of common ions","<p>Corrected — see the coloured notes.</p>\n<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Charge</th><th>Positive ions (cations)</th><th>Negative ions (anions)</th></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>H⁺, Na⁺, K⁺, Li⁺, Ag⁺, <strong>NH₄⁺</strong> (ammonium)</td><td>Cl⁻, Br⁻, I⁻, <strong>OH⁻</strong> (hydroxide), <strong>NO₃⁻</strong> (nitrate), HCO₃⁻</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>2</strong></td><td>Mg²⁺, Ca²⁺, Ba²⁺, Zn²⁺, Fe²⁺ (iron(II)), Cu²⁺ (copper(II))</td><td><strong>O²⁻</strong> (oxide), <strong>S²⁻</strong> (sulfide), SO₄²⁻ (sulfate), SO₃²⁻ (sulfite), CO₃²⁻ (carbonate)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>3</strong></td><td>Al³⁺, Fe³⁺ (iron(III)), Cr³⁺</td><td>N³⁻ (nitride), PO₄³⁻ (phosphate)</td><td>The oxide ion is <strong>O²⁻</strong>, not O₂²⁻, and sulfide is <strong>S²⁻</strong>, not S₂²⁻ (O₂²⁻ would be a peroxide ion). The Roman numeral in a name gives the charge: iron(III) = Fe³⁺.</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-c4-law-relative-masses-compared-w","chem-law",4,"Relative masses compared with carbon-12","<p>Atomic masses are measured <strong>relative to ¹²C = 12 exactly</strong>: on this scale H = 1, He = 4, C = 12, O = 16. (See Ar, item 23.)</p>",0,""],["n-c4-for-reacting-masses-no-moles","chem-form",4,"Reacting masses (no moles)","<div class=\"qn-fbox\"><span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">mass of product</span><span class=\"de\">mass of reactant</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">Mr of product × its number in the equation</span><span class=\"de\">Mr of reactant × its number in the equation</span></span></div><p class=\"qn-fn\">Simple proportion. e.g. CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂: 100 g CaCO₃ gives 56 g CaO, so 25 g gives 14 g. (Mole calculations: Chapter 5.)</p>",0,""],["n-c4-fac-diatomic-elements-h-n-o-f","chem-facts",4,"Name the diatomic elements.","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Diatomic elements: H₂, N₂, O₂, F₂, Cl₂, Br₂, I₂.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Always write them with the 2 in equations.</p></div>",0,""],["n-c4-fac-formulae-you-must-know-nam","chem-facts",4,"Formulae you must know (named in the syllabus)?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Formulae you must know (named in the syllabus)</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>H₂O, CO₂, CO, SO₂, SO₃, NO, NO₂, NH₃, CH₄, HCl, HNO₃, H₂SO₄, NaOH, KOH, Ca(OH)₂, CaO, CaCO₃, NaCl, Fe₂O₃, Al₂O₃, SiO₂, CuSO₄, CoCl₂, AgNO₃, Ba(NO₃)₂, C₂H₅OH, CH₃COOH, C₆H₁₂O₆.</p></div>",0,""],["n-c5-for-amount-of-substance","chem-form",5,"Amount of substance","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">moles = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">mass (g)</span><span class=\"de\">molar mass (g/mol)</span></span></div><p class=\"qn-re\"><span class=\"qn-lab\">Rearranged</span> <b>mass = moles × molar mass</b> · <b>molar mass = mass ÷ moles</b></p><p class=\"qn-fn\">Molar mass = Ar for an element, Mr for a compound.</p>",1,""],["n-c5-for-number-of-particles","chem-form",5,"Number of particles","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">number of particles = moles × 6.02 × 10²³</div><p class=\"qn-fn\">6.02 × 10²³ is the Avogadro constant.</p>",1,""],["n-c5-for-percentage-yield","chem-form",5,"Percentage yield","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">% yield = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">actual yield</span><span class=\"de\">theoretical yield</span></span> × 100</div><p class=\"qn-fn\">The theoretical (predicted) yield is calculated from the equation, using the limiting reactant.</p>",1,""],["n-c5-for-percentage-purity","chem-form",5,"Percentage purity","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">% purity = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">mass of pure substance</span><span class=\"de\">mass of impure sample</span></span> × 100</div>",1,""],["n-c5-for-volume-of-a-gas-at-r-t-p","chem-form",5,"Volume of a gas at r.t.p.","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">volume (dm³) = moles × 24</div><p class=\"qn-re\"><span class=\"qn-lab\">Rearranged</span> <b>moles = volume (dm³) ÷ 24</b></p><p class=\"qn-fn\">With cm³ use 24 000. r.t.p. = room temperature and pressure.</p>",1,""],["n-c5-for-moles-in-a-solution","chem-form",5,"Moles in a solution","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">moles = concentration (mol/dm³) × volume (dm³)</div><p class=\"qn-re\"><span class=\"qn-lab\">Rearranged</span> <b>concentration = moles ÷ volume</b> · <b>volume = moles ÷ concentration</b></p><p class=\"qn-fn\">Volume in dm³ = volume in cm³ ÷ 1000.</p>",1,""],["n-c5-for-concentration-in-g-dm","chem-form",5,"Concentration in g/dm³","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">concentration (g/dm³) = concentration (mol/dm³) × molar mass</div>",1,""],["n-c5-for-reacting-masses-with-moles","chem-form",5,"Reacting masses with moles","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">moles of A → mole ratio → moles of B</div><p class=\"qn-fn\">Step 1: moles of the known substance = mass ÷ Mr. Step 2: use the mole ratio from the balanced equation. Step 3: convert moles of the unknown into mass (× Mr), gas volume (× 24 dm³) or concentration (÷ volume in dm³).</p><p class=\"qn-fn\">e.g. CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂ with 25 g CaCO₃: 25 ÷ 100 = 0.25 mol → 1 : 1 → 0.25 × 56 = 14 g CaO; volume of CO₂ = 0.25 × 24 = 6 dm³.</p>",1,""],["n-c5-for-empirical-formula","chem-form",5,"Empirical formula","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">ratio of atoms = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">mass (or %) of each element</span><span class=\"de\">Ar</span></span>, then ÷ the smallest</div><p class=\"qn-fn\">Then give the simplest whole-number ratio. e.g. 2.4 g Mg + 1.6 g O: 2.4 ÷ 24 = 0.1 and 1.6 ÷ 16 = 0.1 → 1 : 1 → MgO.</p>",1,""],["n-c5-for-molecular-formula","chem-form",5,"Molecular formula","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">n = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">Mr</span><span class=\"de\">empirical formula mass</span></span></div><p class=\"qn-fn\">Molecular formula = (empirical formula) × n.</p>",1,""],["n-c5-for-titration-calculation","chem-form",5,"Titration calculation","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">moles = c × V  →  mole ratio  →  c = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">moles</span><span class=\"de\">volume (dm³)</span></span></div><p class=\"qn-fn\">e.g. 25.0 cm³ NaOH neutralised by 20.0 cm³ of 0.100 mol/dm³ HCl: moles HCl = 0.100 × 0.0200 = 0.00200 → 1 : 1 → c(NaOH) = 0.00200 ÷ 0.0250 = 0.0800 mol/dm³.</p>",1,""],["n-c5-for-water-of-crystallisation","chem-form",5,"Water of crystallisation","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">x = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">moles of water</span><span class=\"de\">moles of anhydrous salt</span></span></div><p class=\"qn-fn\">Heat the hydrated salt to constant mass. Moles of anhydrous salt = mass ÷ Mr; moles of water = mass lost ÷ 18. e.g. CuSO₄·xH₂O gives x = 5.</p>",1,""],["n-c5-law-avogadro-constant-6-02-10","chem-law",5,"Value of the Avogadro constant?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Avogadro constant = 6.02 × 10²³ per mole<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p>",1,"Answer"],["n-c5-law-avogadro-s-law","chem-law",5,"Avogadro's law","<p><strong>Equal volumes of all gases at the same temperature and pressure contain the same number of molecules</strong> — so one mole of <strong>any</strong> gas has the same volume (the molar gas volume).</p>",0,""],["n-c5-law-r-t-p-room-temperature-and","chem-law",5,"What is r.t.p.?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">r.t.p. = room temperature and pressure</p><p><strong>25 °C (298 K) and 101.3 kPa (1 atmosphere).</strong></p>",0,"Answer"],["n-c5-law-molar-gas-volume-24-dm-at","chem-law",5,"Molar gas volume at r.t.p.?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Molar gas volume = 24 dm³ at r.t.p.<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p>",1,"Answer"],["n-c5-fac-the-three-mole-equations","chem-facts",5,"The three mole equations?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">The three mole equations<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p><strong>mass = moles × M</strong>; <strong>moles = concentration × volume (dm³)</strong>; <strong>gas volume (dm³) = moles × 24</strong>. Rearrange the one you need.</p></div>",1,""],["n-c5-fac-always-convert-cm-to-dm-10","chem-facts",5,"What must you do to a volume in cm³ before using c × V?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Always convert cm³ to dm³ (÷ 1000) before using c × V.</p>",0,""],["n-c5-fac-round-only-the-final-answe","chem-facts",5,"When do you round in a calculation?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Round only the final answer; keep at least 3 s.f. in working.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Give the answer to the s.f. of the least precise data (usually 3 s.f.).</p></div>",0,""],["n-c6-fac-electrolysis-of-an-ionic-c","chem-facts",6,"What state must an ionic compound be in for electrolysis?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Electrolysis of an ionic compound needs it to be molten or dissolved — solid ionic compounds don't conduct.</p>",0,""],["n-c6-fac-reduction-always-happens-a","chem-facts",6,"At which electrode do reduction and oxidation happen?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Reduction always happens at the cathode, oxidation at the anode.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p></div>",1,""],["n-c7-for-enthalpy-change-from-bond","chem-form",7,"Enthalpy change from bond energies","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">ΔH = energy taken in breaking bonds − energy given out making bonds</div><p class=\"qn-fn\">Negative ΔH = exothermic; positive ΔH = endothermic.</p><p class=\"qn-fn\">e.g. H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl (H–H 436, Cl–Cl 242, H–Cl 431 kJ/mol): broken 436 + 242 = 678; made 2 × 431 = 862 → ΔH = 678 − 862 = −184 kJ/mol (exothermic).</p>",1,""],["n-c7-fac-a-temperature-rise-means-e","chem-facts",7,"Temperature rise or fall: exothermic or endothermic?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">A temperature rise means exothermic; a fall means endothermic.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Measure with a thermometer in a polystyrene cup.</p></div>",0,""],["n-c8-law-increasing-the-concentrati","chem-law",8,"Effect of concentration on the rate, and why?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Increasing the concentration of a solution increases the rate<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p><p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> More particles <strong>per unit volume</strong> → <strong>more frequent collisions</strong> → more successful collisions per second.</p>",1,"Answer"],["n-c8-law-increasing-the-temperature","chem-law",8,"Effect of temperature on the rate, and why?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Increasing the temperature increases the rate<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p><p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> Particles have <strong>more kinetic energy</strong> → move faster → <strong>more frequent collisions</strong> AND a <strong>greater proportion of collisions have energy ≥ Ea</strong> (more successful collisions).</p>",1,"Answer"],["n-c8-law-increasing-the-pressure-of","chem-law",8,"Effect of gas pressure on the rate, and why?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Increasing the pressure of gases increases the rate<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p><p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> Gas particles are <strong>closer together</strong> (more particles per unit volume) → <strong>more frequent collisions</strong>.</p>",1,"Answer"],["n-c8-law-increasing-the-surface-are","chem-law",8,"Effect of surface area on the rate, and why?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Increasing the surface area of a solid increases the rate<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p><p>Use <strong>smaller pieces / powder</strong>. <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> More particles are <strong>exposed</strong> on the surface → <strong>more frequent collisions</strong>.</p>",1,"Answer"],["n-c8-law-adding-a-catalyst-increase","chem-law",8,"Effect of a catalyst on the rate, and why?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Adding a catalyst increases the rate; removing it decreases the rate<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p><p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> The catalyst <strong>lowers the activation energy</strong>, so a <strong>greater proportion of collisions are successful</strong>.</p>",1,"Answer"],["n-c9-law-le-chatelier-s-principle-p","chem-law",9,"Le Chatelier's principle — predicting the position of equilibrium","<p><strong>If a change is made to a system at equilibrium, the position of equilibrium moves to oppose (reduce) the change.</strong></p>\n<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Change</th><th>Effect on position of equilibrium</th></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Increase temperature</strong></td><td>Moves in the <strong>endothermic</strong> direction</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Decrease temperature</strong></td><td>Moves in the <strong>exothermic</strong> direction</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Increase pressure</strong> (gases)</td><td>Moves to the side with <strong>fewer gas molecules</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Decrease pressure</strong></td><td>Moves to the side with <strong>more gas molecules</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Increase concentration</strong> of a reactant</td><td>Moves to the <strong>right</strong> (more products)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Remove a product</strong></td><td>Moves to the <strong>right</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Add a catalyst</strong></td><td><strong>No change</strong> in position — equilibrium is reached <strong>faster</strong></td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Use the information in the question (which direction is exothermic, number of gas moles).</p>",1,""],["n-c9-tes-chemical-tests-for-water","chem-test",9,"Chemical tests for water","<p><strong>Anhydrous copper(II) sulfate turns from white to blue.</strong> <strong>Anhydrous cobalt(II) chloride turns from blue to pink</strong> (cobalt chloride paper). These show that <strong>water is present</strong> — not that it is pure.</p>",0,""],["n-c9-tes-test-for-the-purity-of-wat","chem-test",9,"Test for the purity of water","<p><strong>Pure water melts at exactly 0 °C and boils at exactly 100 °C</strong> (at atmospheric pressure). Impurities raise the boiling point and lower the melting point.</p>",0,""],["n-c9-fac-equilibrium-rates-are-equa","chem-facts",9,"At equilibrium: what about the rates and the concentrations?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Equilibrium: rates are equal, concentrations are constant (not equal).</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Common mistake.</p></div>",0,""],["n-c9-fac-haber-450-c-200-atm-iron-c","chem-facts",9,"Conditions for the Haber and Contact processes?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Haber: 450 °C, 200 atm, iron. Contact: 450 °C, 2 atm, V₂O₅.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Learn both sets.</p></div>",0,""],["n-c10-tes-tests-for-reducing-and-oxi","chem-test",10,"Tests for reducing and oxidising agents","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Reagent</th><th>What it detects</th><th>Colour change if positive</th><th>Why</th></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Acidified aqueous potassium manganate(VII)</strong> (an <strong>oxidising</strong> agent)</td><td>A <strong>reducing agent</strong></td><td><strong>Purple → colourless</strong></td><td>MnO₄⁻ is <strong>reduced</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Aqueous potassium iodide</strong> (a <strong>reducing</strong> agent)</td><td>An <strong>oxidising agent</strong></td><td><strong>Colourless → brown</strong> (iodine formed)</td><td>I⁻ is <strong>oxidised</strong> to I₂</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>(A drop of starch turns the iodine <strong>blue-black</strong> — not required, but confirms iodine.)</p>",1,""],["n-c10-fac-every-displacement-reactio","chem-facts",10,"What type of reaction is every displacement reaction?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Every displacement reaction of metals and of halogens is a redox reaction.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p></div>",1,""],["n-c10-fac-combustion-rusting-and-res","chem-facts",10,"Combustion, rusting and respiration are which type of reaction?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Combustion, rusting and respiration are oxidation reactions.</p>",0,""],["n-c11-rea-reactions-of-acids","chem-react",11,"Reactions of acids","<p>(a) <strong>acid + metal → salt + hydrogen</strong>: Mg + 2HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂ (fizzing; gas pops with a lighted splint). (b) <strong>acid + base → salt + water</strong>: CuO + H₂SO₄ → CuSO₄ + H₂O; NaOH + HCl → NaCl + H₂O. (c) <strong>acid + carbonate → salt + water + carbon dioxide</strong> (see 77).</p>",0,""],["n-c11-rea-reactions-of-bases","chem-react",11,"Reactions of bases","<p>(a) <strong>base + acid → salt + water</strong> (neutralisation). (b) <strong>base + ammonium salt → salt + water + ammonia</strong>: e.g. 2NH₄Cl + Ca(OH)₂ → CaCl₂ + 2H₂O + 2NH₃ (warm; ammonia turns damp red litmus blue).</p>",0,""],["n-c11-tes-ammonia-turns-damp-red-lit","chem-test",11,"Ammonia turns damp red litmus paper blue","<p>Used to show a base (alkali) has reacted with an ammonium salt — ammonia is the only common <strong>alkaline gas</strong>.</p>",0,""],["n-c11-fac-all-alkalis-are-bases-but","chem-facts",11,"Are all bases alkalis?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">All alkalis are bases, but not all bases are alkalis</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Alkalis are the <strong>soluble</strong> bases. e.g. copper(II) oxide is a base but not an alkali (insoluble).</p></div>",0,""],["n-c11-fac-common-alkali-formulae","chem-facts",11,"Formulae of the common alkalis?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Common alkali formulae</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Sodium hydroxide (caustic soda) <strong>NaOH</strong>; potassium hydroxide (caustic potash) <strong>KOH</strong>; limewater = aqueous calcium hydroxide <strong>Ca(OH)₂</strong> (not \"CaOH\" — calcium is 2+, so two OH⁻); aqueous ammonia <strong>NH₃(aq)</strong> (sometimes written NH₄OH, never \"NH₃OH\").</p></div>",0,""],["n-c11-fac-burning-elements-in-oxygen","chem-facts",11,"Burning elements in oxygen: which oxides form?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Burning elements in oxygen — and the oxides formed</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Non-metals: <strong>sulfur</strong> burns with a <strong>blue</strong> flame → SO₂ (acidic); <strong>carbon</strong> glows <strong>red</strong> → CO₂ (acidic); <strong>phosphorus</strong> burns with a yellow flame → P₄O₁₀ (acidic). Metals: <strong>sodium</strong> yellow flame → Na₂O (basic); <strong>magnesium</strong> brilliant <strong>white</strong> flame → MgO (basic); <strong>calcium</strong> red flame → CaO (basic); <strong>iron</strong> gives <strong>yellow sparks</strong> → iron oxide (basic). <strong>Copper does not burn</strong> — it <strong>turns black</strong> (copper(II) oxide, CuO, basic). There is no \"black flame\".</p></div>",0,""],["n-c12-law-general-solubility-rules-f","chem-law",12,"General solubility rules for salts","<p>(a) <strong>Sodium, potassium and ammonium salts are soluble.</strong> (b) <strong>Nitrates are soluble.</strong> (c) <strong>Chlorides are soluble, except lead and silver.</strong> (d) <strong>Sulfates are soluble, except barium, calcium and lead.</strong> (e) <strong>Carbonates are insoluble, except sodium, potassium and ammonium.</strong> (f) <strong>Hydroxides are insoluble, except sodium, potassium, ammonium and calcium (partially).</strong><br />\nCorrections to your table: the nitrate ion is <strong>NO₃⁻</strong> (charge 1−, not 2−); the soluble carbonate is <strong>ammonium (NH₄⁺)</strong> carbonate, not \"NH₃\". The hydroxide rule (f) was missing.</p>",0,""],["n-c12-rea-preparing-a-soluble-salt-f","chem-react",12,"Preparing a soluble salt from an insoluble reactant — excess metal, base or carbonate","<ol>\n<li><strong>Warm the acid</strong> and add the solid (metal, insoluble base or carbonate) <strong>a little at a time until it is in excess</strong> (no more dissolves / no more fizzing) — this makes sure <strong>all the acid is used up</strong>. 2. <strong>Filter</strong> to remove the excess solid. 3. <strong>Heat the filtrate</strong> to evaporate some water until it is <strong>saturated</strong> (crystals form on a glass rod dipped in and cooled). 4. <strong>Leave to cool and crystallise.</strong> 5. <strong>Filter</strong> off the crystals, <strong>wash</strong> with a little cold distilled water and <strong>dry</strong> between filter papers (or in a warm place).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>e.g. copper(II) sulfate: CuO(s) + H₂SO₄(aq) → CuSO₄(aq) + H₂O(l); zinc chloride from zinc + HCl; magnesium sulfate from MgCO₃ + H₂SO₄.</p>",0,""],["n-c12-rea-preparing-a-soluble-salt-f-2","chem-react",12,"Preparing a soluble salt from an alkali — titration method","<p>Used for <strong>sodium, potassium and ammonium salts</strong> (the reactants are all soluble, so excess can't be filtered off). 1. Use a <strong>volumetric pipette</strong> to put a known volume of alkali in a conical flask with a few drops of <strong>indicator</strong> (methyl orange or thymolphthalein). 2. Add acid from a <strong>burette</strong> until the indicator <strong>just changes colour</strong> (end-point). Note the volume. 3. <strong>Repeat without the indicator</strong> using the same volumes (so the salt is pure). 4. Evaporate and crystallise as above. e.g. NaOH + HCl → NaCl + H₂O.</p>",0,""],["n-c12-rea-preparing-an-insoluble-sal","chem-react",12,"Preparing an insoluble salt — precipitation","<ol>\n<li><strong>Mix solutions of two soluble salts</strong> that between them contain the ions of the insoluble salt (e.g. barium chloride + sodium sulfate → barium sulfate). 2. <strong>Filter</strong> to collect the precipitate. 3. <strong>Wash</strong> it with distilled water (removes soluble impurities). 4. <strong>Dry</strong> it (warm oven / between filter papers).</li>\n</ol>",1,""],["n-c12-fac-calcium-carbonate-caco-occ","chem-facts",12,"Calcium carbonate occurs naturally as…?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Calcium carbonate (CaCO₃) occurs as limestone, marble and chalk</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Limestone is used in the blast furnace (Ch 16) and to make calcium oxide.</p></div>",0,""],["n-c12-fac-colours-of-some-salts","chem-facts",12,"Colours of some salts?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Colours of some salts</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p><strong>Hydrated copper(II) sulfate CuSO₄·5H₂O is blue</strong> (anhydrous: <strong>white</strong>); <strong>hydrated cobalt(II) chloride CoCl₂·6H₂O is pink</strong> (anhydrous: <strong>blue</strong>); <strong>iron(II) sulfate</strong> (FeSO₄) is green; <strong>lead(II) iodide PbI₂</strong> is a <strong>yellow precipitate</strong>; Group I, Group II and zinc salts are white. Your formulae \"Fe₂SO₄\", \"Co₂Cl₂\" and \"lead₂ iodide\" were wrong — iron(II) sulfate is <strong>FeSO₄</strong> and cobalt(II) chloride is <strong>CoCl₂</strong>, because Fe²⁺/Co²⁺ need only one SO₄²⁻ or two Cl⁻.</p></div>",0,""],["n-c13-law-group-names","chem-law",13,"Group names","<p><strong>Group I — alkali metals</strong> (Li, Na, K…). Group II — alkaline earth metals. <strong>Group VII — halogens</strong> (Cl, Br, I…). <strong>Group VIII — noble gases</strong>.</p>",0,""],["n-c13-law-transition-elements-lie-be","chem-law",13,"Where are the transition elements in the Periodic Table?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Transition elements lie between Group II and Group III</p><p>They are all metals.</p>",0,"Answer"],["n-c13-law-patterns-you-can-use-to-pr","chem-law",13,"Patterns you can use to predict properties","<p><strong>Across a period</strong>: elements change from <strong>metals to non-metals</strong> (left to right). <strong>Group number → charge of ion</strong>: Group I <strong>+1</strong>, II <strong>+2</strong>, III <strong>+3</strong>, V <strong>−3</strong>, VI <strong>−2</strong>, VII <strong>−1</strong>. <strong>Elements in the same group have similar chemical properties because they have the same number of outer-shell electrons</strong> (same outer electronic configuration). Use the position (group, metal/non-metal) and the trends to <strong>predict</strong> properties of unfamiliar elements.</p>",0,""],["n-c13-rea-alkali-metals-cold-water-m","chem-react",13,"Alkali metals + cold water: products and observations?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Alkali metals + cold water → metal hydroxide + hydrogen</p><p>e.g. <strong>2Na(s) + 2H₂O(l) → 2NaOH(aq) + H₂(g)</strong>. Observations: <strong>floats, fizzes (bubbles), moves around, melts into a ball</strong> (Na, K), <strong>gets smaller and disappears</strong>; <strong>potassium</strong> also burns with a <strong>lilac</strong> flame; the solution becomes <strong>alkaline</strong> (universal indicator → purple). Reactivity: <strong>Li &lt; Na &lt; K</strong>.</p>",0,"Answer"],["n-c13-rea-chlorine-potassium-iodide","chem-react",13,"Chlorine + potassium iodide","<p><strong>Cl₂(aq) + 2KI(aq) → 2KCl(aq) + I₂(aq)</strong> — solution turns from colourless to <strong>brown</strong> (iodine). Chlorine is more reactive than iodine so it displaces it. <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> Ionic: Cl₂ + 2I⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + I₂.</p>",0,""],["n-c13-fac-periodic-trends-summary","chem-facts",13,"Summary of the Periodic Table trends?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Periodic trends summary</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p><strong>Across a period:</strong> metallic → non-metallic. <strong>Down Group I:</strong> m.p./b.p. <strong>decrease</strong>, metals get <strong>softer</strong>, density <strong>increases</strong>, reactivity <strong>increases</strong>. <strong>Down Group VII:</strong> density and m.p. <strong>increase</strong> (gas → liquid → solid), reactivity <strong>decreases</strong>. <strong>Down any group:</strong> atoms get larger and more metallic.</p></div>",0,""],["n-c14-rea-metals-oxygen-air-metal-ox","chem-react",14,"Metals + oxygen: product and examples?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Metals + oxygen (air) → metal oxide</p><p>e.g. 2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO (bright white flame); 2Cu + O₂ → 2CuO (turns black, no flame). Metal oxides are <strong>basic</strong>.</p>",0,"Answer"],["n-c14-rea-metals-water-steam","chem-react",14,"Metals + water/steam","<p><strong>Reactive metal + cold water → metal hydroxide + hydrogen</strong>: 2Na + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + H₂; Ca + 2H₂O → Ca(OH)₂ + H₂. <strong>Less reactive metal + steam → metal oxide + hydrogen</strong>: Mg + H₂O(g) → MgO + H₂.</p>",0,""],["n-c14-rea-metals-dilute-acids-salt-h","chem-react",14,"Metals + dilute acids: products and observations?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Metals + dilute acids → salt + hydrogen</p><p>e.g. Mg + 2HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂; Zn + H₂SO₄ → ZnSO₄ + H₂. Fizzing; gas pops with a lighted splint. <strong>Copper, silver and gold do not react</strong> (below hydrogen in the reactivity series).</p>",0,"Answer"],["n-c14-law-composition-of-alloys","chem-law",14,"Composition of alloys","<p><strong>Brass = copper + zinc.</strong> <strong>Stainless steel = iron + chromium, nickel and carbon.</strong> These two are the ones the syllabus names. Bronze (Cu + Sn), mild steel (Fe + C) and solder (Sn + Pb) are not required.</p>",0,""],["n-c14-use-aluminium","chem-uses",14,"Aluminium","<p><strong>Aircraft</strong> — <strong>low density</strong>. <strong>Overhead electrical cables</strong> — <strong>low density</strong> and <strong>good electrical conductivity</strong>. <strong>Food containers</strong> — <strong>resistance to corrosion</strong> (protected by its oxide layer).</p>",0,""],["n-c14-use-copper","chem-uses",14,"Copper","<p><strong>Electrical wiring</strong> — <strong>good electrical conductivity</strong> and <strong>ductility</strong> (can be drawn into wires).</p>",0,""],["n-c14-use-zinc","chem-uses",14,"Zinc","<p><strong>Galvanising</strong> iron and steel to stop rusting (Chapter 16)<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>.</p>",0,""],["n-c14-use-stainless-steel","chem-uses",14,"Stainless steel","<p><strong>Cutlery</strong> — because of its <strong>hardness</strong> and <strong>resistance to rusting</strong>.</p>",0,""],["n-c14-fac-an-alloy-is-a-mixture-not","chem-facts",14,"Is an alloy a mixture or a compound?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">An alloy is a mixture, not a compound.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>The metals are not chemically combined.</p></div>",0,""],["n-c15-rea-metals-with-water","chem-react",15,"Metals with water","<p><strong>Potassium, sodium and calcium react with cold water</strong> → metal hydroxide + hydrogen. <strong>K</strong> — very vigorous, melts, lilac flame; <strong>Na</strong> — vigorous, fizzes, melts into a ball, moves on the surface; <strong>Ca</strong> — steady fizzing, sinks, cloudy solution (Ca(OH)₂ is only slightly soluble). <strong>Magnesium reacts very slowly with cold water but quickly with steam</strong>: Mg + H₂O(g) → MgO + H₂ (white glow; white solid).</p>",0,""],["n-c15-rea-metals-with-air-oxygen","chem-react",15,"Metals with air/oxygen","<p>K, Na, Ca, Mg, Al, Zn, Fe burn (more reactive metals more vigorously) to form oxides; copper only forms a black oxide layer; silver and gold do not react.</p>",0,""],["n-c15-rea-metals-with-dilute-hydroch","chem-react",15,"Metals with dilute hydrochloric acid","<p><strong>Magnesium</strong> — very fast fizzing; <strong>zinc</strong> — moderate; <strong>iron</strong> — slow; <strong>copper, silver, gold — no reaction</strong> (below hydrogen). e.g. Fe + 2HCl → FeCl₂ + H₂.</p>",0,""],["n-c15-rea-metals-with-dilute-sulfuri","chem-react",15,"Metals with dilute sulfuric acid","<p>Same pattern as hydrochloric acid; the salt is a <strong>sulfate</strong>, e.g. Zn + H₂SO₄ → ZnSO₄ + H₂.</p>",0,""],["n-c15-rea-the-silver-tree-displaceme","chem-react",15,"The \"silver tree\" displacement","<p>A strip of <strong>copper</strong> in <strong>silver nitrate</strong> solution: <strong>Cu(s) + 2AgNO₃(aq) → Cu(NO₃)₂(aq) + 2Ag(s)</strong> — crystals of silver grow on the copper and the solution turns <strong>blue</strong>. Shows copper is more reactive than silver.</p>",0,""],["n-c15-fac-the-reactivity-series","chem-facts",15,"The reactivity series?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">The reactivity series</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p><strong>Potassium, sodium, calcium, magnesium, aluminium, (carbon), zinc, iron, (hydrogen), copper, silver, gold</strong> — most → least reactive. This is the exact syllabus list. Your list also had tin and lead (between iron and hydrogen) — correct, but not required. Memory aid: <strong>P</strong>lease <strong>S</strong>end <strong>C</strong>harlie's <strong>M</strong>onkeys <strong>A</strong>nd <strong>C</strong>ute <strong>Z</strong>ebras <strong>I</strong>nto <strong>H</strong>ot <strong>C</strong>ountries <strong>S</strong>igned <strong>G</strong>ordon.</p></div>",0,""],["n-c16-rea-extraction-of-iron-in-the","chem-react",16,"Extraction of iron in the blast furnace","<p>Raw materials: <strong>hematite, coke (carbon), limestone</strong> (and hot <strong>air</strong>).<br />\n(a) <strong>Burning coke</strong> provides <strong>heat</strong> and makes carbon dioxide: <strong>C + O₂ → CO₂</strong>.<br />\n(b) <strong>Carbon dioxide is reduced</strong> to carbon monoxide by more coke: <strong>C + CO₂ → 2CO</strong>.<br />\n(c) <strong>Carbon monoxide reduces iron(III) oxide</strong> to iron: <strong>Fe₂O₃ + 3CO → 2Fe + 3CO₂</strong>. Molten iron collects at the bottom.<br />\n(d) <strong>Limestone thermally decomposes</strong>: <strong>CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂</strong>.<br />\n(e) <strong>Slag forms</strong>: <strong>CaO + SiO₂ → CaSiO₃</strong>.<br />\n(Symbol equations are Supplement; Core students describe the steps in words.)</p>",1,""],["n-c16-rea-extraction-of-aluminium-by","chem-react",16,"Extraction of aluminium by electrolysis","<p><strong>Aluminium is extracted from purified bauxite (aluminium oxide) by electrolysis</strong> — it is too reactive to be reduced by carbon. Electrolyte: <strong>aluminium oxide dissolved in molten cryolite</strong>; <strong>graphite (carbon) electrodes</strong>.<br />\n<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> <strong>Role of cryolite:</strong> it <strong>lowers the melting point</strong> of the electrolyte (from about 2000 °C to about 950 °C), saving energy, and improves conductivity.<br />\n<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> <strong>Cathode (−):</strong> <strong>Al³⁺ + 3e⁻ → Al</strong> (molten aluminium collects at the bottom). <strong>Anode (+):</strong> <strong>2O²⁻ → O₂ + 4e⁻</strong>.<br />\n(Details of purifying bauxite are not required.)</p>",1,""],["n-c16-law-the-carbon-anodes-must-be","chem-law",16,"The carbon anodes must be replaced regularly","<p>At the high temperature the <strong>oxygen made at the anodes reacts with the carbon</strong> anodes to form <strong>carbon dioxide</strong> (C + O₂ → CO₂), so the anodes <strong>burn away</strong>.</p>",1,""],["n-c16-law-least-reactive-metals-copp","chem-law",16,"Least reactive metals — copper, silver, gold","<p>These are the easiest to obtain; <strong>gold and silver are found uncombined</strong> (as the element) because they are so unreactive.</p>",0,""],["n-c16-law-ease-of-extraction-depends","chem-law",16,"Ease of extraction depends on reactivity","<p><strong>Metals high in the reactivity series</strong> (K, Na, Ca, Mg, <strong>Al</strong>) form very stable compounds — extracted by <strong>electrolysis</strong> of the molten compound (expensive: much electricity). <strong>Metals below carbon</strong> (Zn, <strong>Fe</strong>, Pb, Cu) are extracted by <strong>heating the oxide with carbon</strong> (reduction). <strong>Unreactive metals</strong> (Ag, Au) are found <strong>uncombined</strong>.</p>",0,""],["n-c16-fac-rust-is-hydrated-iron-iii","chem-facts",16,"What is rust, and which metals rust?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Rust is hydrated iron(III) oxide; only iron and steel rust.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Other metals corrode but do not \"rust\".</p></div>",0,""],["n-c16-fac-in-the-blast-furnace-carbo","chem-facts",16,"Main reducing agent in the blast furnace?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">In the blast furnace, carbon monoxide is the main reducing agent.</p>",0,""],["n-c17-fac-complete-combustion-co-h-o","chem-facts",17,"Products of complete and incomplete combustion?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Complete combustion → CO₂ + H₂O; incomplete combustion → CO and/or C (soot) + H₂O.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Incomplete combustion happens when oxygen is limited.</p></div>",0,""],["n-c18-for-general-formula-of-the-alk","chem-form",18,"General formula of the alkanes","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">CₙH₂ₙ₊₂</div><p class=\"qn-fn\">e.g. propane C₃H₈.</p>",0,""],["n-c18-for-general-formula-of-the-alk-2","chem-form",18,"General formula of the alkenes","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">CₙH₂ₙ</div><p class=\"qn-fn\">e.g. ethene C₂H₄.</p>",0,""],["n-c18-for-general-formula-of-the-alc","chem-form",18,"General formula of the alcohols","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">CₙH₂ₙ₊₁OH</div><p class=\"qn-fn\">e.g. ethanol C₂H₅OH.</p>",0,""],["n-c18-for-general-formula-of-the-car","chem-form",18,"General formula of the carboxylic acids","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">CₙH₂ₙ₊₁COOH</div><p class=\"qn-fn\">e.g. ethanoic acid CH₃COOH. Here n counts the carbons before COOH, so ethanoic acid has n = 1.</p>",0,""],["n-c18-fac-don-t-confuse-isomer-same","chem-facts",18,"Isomer compared with isotope?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Don't confuse isomer (same formula, different structure) with isotope (same element, different neutrons).</p>",0,""],["n-c18-fac-ane-all-single-bonds-satur","chem-facts",18,"‑ane and ‑ene: which bonds?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">\"‑ane\" = all single bonds (saturated); \"‑ene\" = C=C (unsaturated).</p>",0,""],["n-c19-rea-cracking-decane","chem-react",19,"Cracking decane","<p>e.g. <strong>C₁₀H₂₂ → C₈H₁₈ + C₂H₄</strong> (octane for petrol + ethene); or C₁₀H₂₂ → C₅H₁₂ + C₂H₄ + C₃H₆. Check: carbons and hydrogens balance; at least one product is an alkene. Conditions: <strong>high temperature (≈ 500 °C) and a catalyst</strong> (aluminium oxide / silica).</p>",0,""],["n-c19-rea-making-ethanol-industriall","chem-react",19,"Making ethanol industrially — hydration of ethene","<p><strong>C₂H₄(g) + H₂O(g) → C₂H₅OH(g)</strong> at <strong>300 °C, 60 atm (6000 kPa), acid catalyst</strong>.</p>",0,""],["n-c19-rea-making-ethanoic-acid","chem-react",19,"Making ethanoic acid","<p>(a) <strong>Oxidation of ethanol with acidified aqueous potassium manganate(VII)</strong> (warm): C₂H₅OH + 2[O] → CH₃COOH + H₂O — the manganate(VII) turns <strong>purple → colourless</strong>. (b) <strong>Bacterial oxidation during vinegar production</strong>: ethanol in wine/beer is oxidised by bacteria in air to ethanoic acid.</p>",1,""],["n-c19-rea-combustion-of-alkanes","chem-react",19,"Combustion of alkanes","<p>Alkanes are <strong>generally unreactive, except for combustion and substitution by chlorine</strong>. <strong>Complete combustion</strong> (plenty of oxygen) → <strong>carbon dioxide + water</strong>: CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O. <strong>Incomplete combustion</strong> (limited oxygen) → <strong>carbon monoxide</strong> and/or <strong>carbon (soot)</strong> + water.</p>",0,""],["n-c19-rea-combustion-of-ethanol","chem-react",19,"Combustion of ethanol","<p>Ethanol burns with a clean blue flame: <strong>C₂H₅OH + 3O₂ → 2CO₂ + 3H₂O</strong> (exothermic — used as a fuel).</p>",0,""],["n-c19-rea-reactions-of-ethanoic-acid","chem-react",19,"Reactions of ethanoic acid","<p>It is a <strong>weak acid</strong> with the usual acid reactions; the salts are <strong>ethanoates</strong> (CH₃COO⁻). With <strong>metals</strong>: 2CH₃COOH + Mg → (CH₃COO)₂Mg + H₂ (<strong>magnesium ethanoate</strong>; slow fizzing). With <strong>bases</strong>: CH₃COOH + NaOH → CH₃COONa + H₂O (<strong>sodium ethanoate</strong>). With <strong>carbonates</strong>: 2CH₃COOH + Na₂CO₃ → 2CH₃COONa + H₂O + CO₂ (fizzing).</p>",0,""],["n-c19-use-uses-of-ethanol","chem-uses",19,"Uses of ethanol","<p><strong>(a) A solvent</strong> (e.g. in perfumes, inks, medicines). <strong>(b) A fuel</strong> (burns cleanly; can be mixed with petrol).</p>",0,""],["n-c19-fac-alkanes-substitution-with","chem-facts",19,"Typical reactions of alkanes and of alkenes?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Alkanes: substitution (with Cl₂ in UV). Alkenes: addition.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p></div>",1,""],["n-c19-fac-bromine-water-orange-colou","chem-facts",19,"Bromine water test for C=C?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Bromine water: orange → colourless = C=C present.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Exam favourite.</p></div>",0,""],["n-c20-use-petroleum-fractions-and-th","chem-uses",20,"Petroleum fractions and their uses","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Fraction (top → bottom)</th><th>Use</th></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Refinery gas</strong></td><td><strong>Gas used in heating and cooking</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Gasoline / petrol</strong></td><td><strong>Fuel used in cars</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Naphtha</strong></td><td><strong>Chemical feedstock</strong> (to make other chemicals, e.g. by cracking)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Kerosene / paraffin</strong></td><td><strong>Jet fuel</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Diesel oil / gas oil</strong></td><td><strong>Fuel used in diesel engines</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Fuel oil</strong></td><td><strong>Fuel used in ships and home heating systems</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Lubricating oil</strong></td><td><strong>Lubricants, waxes and polishes</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Bitumen</strong></td><td><strong>Making roads</strong></td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>(Carbon-chain lengths and boiling temperatures are not required.)</p>",0,""],["n-c20-fac-proteins-and-nylon-both-ha","chem-facts",20,"Which link is in proteins and nylon, and which in PET?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Proteins and nylon both have amide links; PET has ester links.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p></div>",1,""],["n-c21-fac-draw-the-chromatography-ba","chem-facts",21,"Chromatography baseline: drawn with what, and where?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Draw the chromatography baseline in pencil and keep it above the solvent level.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Ink would separate; spots would dissolve into the solvent.</p></div>",0,""],["n-c21-fac-in-distillation-the-thermo","chem-facts",21,"Distillation: thermometer bulb position and condenser water flow?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">In distillation, the thermometer bulb sits at the side-arm and water enters the condenser at the bottom.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Common diagram-labelling marks.</p></div>",0,""],["n-c22-rea-reactions-of-cations-with","chem-react",22,"Reactions of cations with aqueous sodium hydroxide","<p>Add a few drops, then <strong>excess</strong>.</p>\n<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Cation</th><th>Effect of aqueous sodium hydroxide</th></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Aluminium, Al³⁺</strong></td><td><strong>White ppt., soluble in excess, giving a colourless solution</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Ammonium, NH₄⁺</strong></td><td><strong>Ammonia produced on warming</strong> (turns damp red litmus blue)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Calcium, Ca²⁺</strong></td><td><strong>White ppt., insoluble in excess</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Chromium(III), Cr³⁺</strong></td><td><strong>Green ppt., soluble in excess</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Copper(II), Cu²⁺</strong></td><td><strong>Light blue ppt., insoluble in excess</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Iron(II), Fe²⁺</strong></td><td><strong>Green ppt., insoluble in excess, ppt. turns brown near surface on standing</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Iron(III), Fe³⁺</strong></td><td><strong>Red-brown ppt., insoluble in excess</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Zinc, Zn²⁺</strong></td><td><strong>White ppt., soluble in excess, giving a colourless solution</strong></td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-c22-rea-reactions-of-cations-with-2","chem-react",22,"Reactions of cations with aqueous ammonia","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Cation</th><th>Effect of aqueous ammonia</th></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Aluminium, Al³⁺</strong></td><td><strong>White ppt., insoluble in excess</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Ammonium, NH₄⁺</strong></td><td>—</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Calcium, Ca²⁺</strong></td><td><strong>No ppt. or very slight white ppt.</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Chromium(III), Cr³⁺</strong></td><td><strong>Green ppt., insoluble in excess</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Copper(II), Cu²⁺</strong></td><td><strong>Light blue ppt., soluble in excess, giving a dark blue solution</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Iron(II), Fe²⁺</strong></td><td><strong>Green ppt., insoluble in excess, ppt. turns brown near surface on standing</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Iron(III), Fe³⁺</strong></td><td><strong>Red-brown ppt., insoluble in excess</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Zinc, Zn²⁺</strong></td><td><strong>White ppt., soluble in excess, giving a colourless solution</strong></td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-c22-tes-tests-for-anions","chem-test",22,"Tests for anions","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Anion</th><th>Test</th><th>Result</th></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Carbonate, CO₃²⁻</strong></td><td>Add <strong>dilute acid</strong>, then test the gas with limewater</td><td><strong>Effervescence; carbon dioxide produced</strong> (limewater turns milky)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Chloride, Cl⁻</strong></td><td>Acidify with <strong>dilute nitric acid</strong>, then add <strong>aqueous silver nitrate</strong></td><td><strong>White ppt.</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Bromide, Br⁻</strong></td><td>Acidify with dilute nitric acid, then add aqueous silver nitrate</td><td><strong>Cream ppt.</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Iodide, I⁻</strong></td><td>Acidify with dilute nitric acid, then add aqueous silver nitrate</td><td><strong>Yellow ppt.</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Sulfate, SO₄²⁻</strong></td><td>Acidify with <strong>dilute nitric acid</strong>, then add <strong>aqueous barium nitrate</strong></td><td><strong>White ppt.</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Sulfite, SO₃²⁻</strong></td><td>Add a small volume of <strong>acidified aqueous potassium manganate(VII)</strong></td><td><strong>Purple → colourless</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Nitrate, NO₃⁻</strong></td><td>Add <strong>aqueous sodium hydroxide</strong>, then <strong>aluminium foil</strong>; <strong>warm</strong> carefully</td><td><strong>Ammonia produced</strong> (damp red litmus → blue)</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>The acid is added first to <strong>remove carbonate ions</strong>, which would also give a precipitate. Use nitric acid (not hydrochloric) for the chloride test, otherwise you add chloride ions yourself.</p>",0,""],["n-c22-tes-identifying-cations","chem-test",22,"Identifying cations","<p>Use the two tables above: first add <strong>NaOH(aq)</strong> drop by drop then in excess; then a fresh sample with <strong>NH₃(aq)</strong>. <strong>Al³⁺ vs Zn²⁺</strong>: both white with NaOH and soluble in excess — <strong>zinc's ppt. dissolves in excess ammonia, aluminium's doesn't</strong>. <strong>Ca²⁺</strong>: white with NaOH but (almost) nothing with ammonia.</p>",0,""],["n-c22-tes-tests-for-gases","chem-test",22,"Tests for gases","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Gas</th><th>Test</th><th>Result</th></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Ammonia, NH₃</strong></td><td><strong>Damp red litmus paper</strong></td><td>Turns <strong>blue</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Carbon dioxide, CO₂</strong></td><td>Bubble through <strong>limewater</strong></td><td>Turns <strong>milky</strong> (cloudy white)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Chlorine, Cl₂</strong></td><td><strong>Damp litmus paper</strong></td><td><strong>Bleached</strong> (turns white; blue litmus goes red first)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Hydrogen, H₂</strong></td><td><strong>Lighted splint</strong></td><td><strong>'Pops'</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Oxygen, O₂</strong></td><td><strong>Glowing splint</strong></td><td><strong>Relights</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Sulfur dioxide, SO₂</strong></td><td><strong>Acidified aqueous potassium manganate(VII)</strong></td><td><strong>Purple → colourless</strong></td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-c22-tes-flame-tests-for-metal-ions","chem-test",22,"Flame tests for metal ions","<p>Dip a clean nichrome/platinum wire (cleaned in concentrated HCl) into the solid, hold it in a <strong>roaring blue Bunsen flame</strong>.</p>\n<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Metal ion</th><th>Flame colour</th></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Lithium, Li⁺</strong></td><td><strong>Red</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Sodium, Na⁺</strong></td><td><strong>Yellow</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Potassium, K⁺</strong></td><td><strong>Lilac</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Calcium, Ca²⁺</strong></td><td><strong>Orange-red</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Barium, Ba²⁺</strong></td><td><strong>Light green</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Copper(II), Cu²⁺</strong></td><td><strong>Blue-green</strong></td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-c22-fac-ppt-precipitate-always-say","chem-facts",22,"What must you always say about a ppt.?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">\"ppt.\" = precipitate; always say the colour and what happens in excess.</p>",0,""]],"defs":[["d-c1-mixture",1,"Mixture","<p>Two or more substances mixed together but not chemically combined.</p>","<p>The substances keep their own properties and can be separated by <strong>physical methods</strong> (filtration, distillation, chromatography…). Composition can vary.</p>",0],["d-c1-solvent",1,"Solvent","<p>A substance that dissolves a solute.</p>","<p>(e.g. water, ethanol).</p>",0],["d-c1-solute",1,"Solute","<p>A substance that is dissolved in a solvent.</p>","",0],["d-c1-solution",1,"Solution","<p>A mixture of one or more solutes dissolved in a solvent.</p>","",0],["d-c1-saturated-solution",1,"Saturated solution","<p>A solution containing the maximum concentration of a solute dissolved in the solvent at a specified temperature.</p>","",0],["d-c1-residue",1,"Residue","<p>A substance that remains after evaporation, distillation, filtration or any similar process.</p>","",0],["d-c1-filtrate",1,"Filtrate","<p>A liquid or solution that has passed through a filter.</p>","",0],["d-c2-element",2,"Element","<p>A substance that cannot be broken down into simpler substances by chemical methods.</p>","<p>All its atoms have the <strong>same number of protons</strong> (same proton number). e.g. iron, oxygen, carbon.</p>",0],["d-c2-compound",2,"Compound","<p>A substance made of two or more elements chemically combined in fixed proportions.</p>","<p>It has <strong>different properties</strong> from its elements and can only be separated by <strong>chemical reactions</strong>. e.g. water H₂O, sodium chloride NaCl.</p>",0],["d-c2-isotopes",2,"Isotopes","<p>Different atoms of the same element that have the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons.</p><p>Isotopes of the same element have the same chemical properties because they have the same number of electrons and therefore the same electronic configuration.</p>","<p>e.g. ¹²C and ¹⁴C; ³⁵Cl and ³⁷Cl.</p>",0],["d-c2-proton-number-atomic-number-z",2,"Proton number / atomic number (Z)","<p>The number of protons in the nucleus of an atom.</p>","<p>(It decides which element it is and its position in the Periodic Table.)</p>",0],["d-c2-mass-number-nucleon-number-a",2,"Mass number / nucleon number (A)","<p>The total number of protons and neutrons in the nucleus of an atom.</p>","",0],["d-c2-relative-atomic-mass-ar",2,"Relative atomic mass, Ar","<p>The average mass of the isotopes of an element compared to 1/12th of the mass of an atom of ¹²C.</p>","<p>It is not a whole number for elements with several isotopes (e.g. Cl = 35.5).</p>",0],["d-c2-atom-and-its-structure",2,"Atom and its structure","<p>The atom has a central nucleus containing neutrons and protons, surrounded by electrons in shells.</p>","",0],["d-c3-ionic-bond",3,"Ionic bond","<p>A strong electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions.</p>","<p>Formed when a <strong>metal atom transfers electrons to a non-metal atom</strong>, so both get a full outer shell (noble gas configuration).</p>",0],["d-c3-covalent-bond",3,"Covalent bond","<p>Formed when a pair of electrons is shared between two atoms, leading to noble gas electronic configurations.</p>","<p>Usually between <strong>non-metal</strong> atoms. A <strong>double bond</strong> = two shared pairs (e.g. O=O, C=O, C=C); a <strong>triple bond</strong> = three shared pairs (N≡N).</p>",0],["d-c3-metallic-bonding",3,"Metallic bonding<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The electrostatic attraction between the positive ions in a giant metallic lattice and a 'sea' of delocalised electrons.</p>","",1],["d-c3-cation-and-anion",3,"Cation and anion","<p>Positive ions are called cations — formed when atoms (metals) lose electrons.</p><p>Negative ions are called anions — formed when atoms (non-metals) gain electrons.</p>","<p>e.g. Na → Na⁺ + e⁻. e.g. Cl + e⁻ → Cl⁻.</p>",0],["d-c3-giant-ionic-lattice",3,"Giant ionic lattice<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A regular arrangement of alternating positive and negative ions.</p>","<p>Held together by strong electrostatic attraction in all directions (e.g. NaCl: each Na⁺ surrounded by 6 Cl⁻ and each Cl⁻ by 6 Na⁺).</p>",1],["d-c4-molecular-formula",4,"Molecular formula","<p>The number and type of different atoms in one molecule.</p>","<p>e.g. ethane C₂H₆, glucose C₆H₁₂O₆.</p>",0],["d-c4-empirical-formula",4,"Empirical formula<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The simplest whole number ratio of the different atoms or ions in a compound.</p>","<p>e.g. ethane C₂H₆ → <strong>CH₃</strong>; glucose → <strong>CH₂O</strong>. For ionic compounds the formula <em>is</em> the empirical formula (NaCl, MgO).</p>",1],["d-c4-relative-molecular-mass-mr",4,"Relative molecular mass, Mr","<p>The sum of the relative atomic masses.</p>","<p>Add up the Ar of all the atoms in one molecule (or formula unit). e.g. H₂O = 2(1) + 16 = <strong>18</strong>. No units.</p>",0],["d-c4-relative-formula-mass-mr",4,"Relative formula mass, Mr","<p>The sum of the relative atomic masses.</p>","<p>Of all the atoms in the formula — the term <strong>used for ionic compounds</strong> (which have no molecules). e.g. CaCO₃ = 40 + 12 + 3(16) = <strong>100</strong>; Ca(OH)₂ = 40 + 2(16 + 1) = <strong>74</strong>.</p>",0],["d-c5-avogadro-constant",5,"Avogadro constant<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The number of particles in one mole: 6.02 × 10²³.</p>","<p>The particles can be atoms, ions or molecules.</p>",1],["d-c5-mole-mol",5,"Mole (mol)<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The unit of amount of substance; one mole contains 6.02 × 10²³ particles.</p>","<p>e.g. atoms, ions, molecules (the Avogadro constant). One mole of a substance has a mass equal to its <strong>Ar or Mr in grams</strong>.</p>",1],["d-c6-electrolysis",6,"Electrolysis","<p>The decomposition of an ionic compound, when molten or in aqueous solution, by the passage of an electric current.</p>","",0],["d-c6-electrolyte",6,"Electrolyte","<p>The molten or aqueous substance that undergoes electrolysis.</p>","<p>It conducts because its <strong>ions are free to move</strong>.</p>",0],["d-c6-electrolytic-cell-anode-and-ca",6,"Electrolytic cell — anode and cathode","<p>The anode is the positive electrode; the cathode is the negative electrode.</p>","<p>The apparatus for electrolysis: an electrolyte and two <strong>electrodes</strong> connected to a d.c. supply. <strong>Inert electrodes</strong> (graphite/carbon or platinum) do not react.</p>",0],["d-c7-endothermic-reaction",7,"Endothermic reaction","<p>A reaction that takes in thermal energy from the surroundings, leading to a decrease in the temperature of the surroundings.</p>","<p>e.g. thermal decomposition, photosynthesis, dissolving ammonium nitrate. <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> <strong>ΔH is positive.</strong></p>",0],["d-c7-exothermic-reaction",7,"Exothermic reaction","<p>A reaction that transfers thermal energy to the surroundings, leading to an increase in the temperature of the surroundings.</p>","<p>e.g. combustion, neutralisation, respiration, metals with acids. <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> <strong>ΔH is negative.</strong></p>",0],["d-c7-enthalpy-change-h",7,"Enthalpy change, ΔH<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The transfer of thermal energy during a reaction is called the enthalpy change, ΔH, of the reaction. ΔH is negative for exothermic reactions and positive for endothermic reactions.</p>","<p>Units: <strong>kJ/mol</strong>.</p>",1],["d-c7-activation-energy-ea",7,"Activation energy, Ea<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The minimum energy that colliding particles must have to react.</p>","",1],["d-c7-bond-breaking-and-bond-making",7,"Bond breaking and bond making<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Bond breaking is an endothermic process; bond making is an exothermic process.</p>","<p>A reaction is <strong>exothermic</strong> if <strong>more energy is released making the new bonds</strong> in the products than is taken in breaking the bonds in the reactants; <strong>endothermic</strong> if more energy is taken in breaking bonds than released making them.</p>",1],["d-c8-catalyst",8,"Catalyst<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A substance that increases the rate of a reaction and is unchanged at the end of the reaction.</p><p>A catalyst decreases the activation energy, Ea, of a reaction.</p>","<p>It provides a different reaction pathway with a lower Ea, so more collisions are successful.</p>",1],["d-c9-equilibrium",9,"Equilibrium<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A reversible reaction in a closed system is at equilibrium when (a) the rate of the forward reaction is equal to the rate of the reverse reaction and (b) the concentrations of reactants and products are no longer changing.</p>","<p>Both reactions are still happening (dynamic equilibrium). Concentrations are constant — <strong>not</strong> necessarily equal.</p>",1],["d-c10-redox-reaction",10,"Redox reaction","<p>A reaction involving simultaneous oxidation and reduction.</p>","<p>One substance is oxidised while another is reduced.</p>",0],["d-c10-oxidation-and-reduction-the-th",10,"Oxidation and reduction — the three definitions<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>W</p>","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th></th><th>Oxidation</th><th>Reduction</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Oxygen [Core]</td><td><strong>Gain of oxygen</strong></td><td><strong>Loss of oxygen</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Electrons <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></td><td><strong>Loss of electrons</strong></td><td><strong>Gain of electrons</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Oxidation number <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></td><td><strong>Increase in oxidation number</strong></td><td><strong>Decrease in oxidation number</strong></td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Memory aid: <strong>OIL RIG</strong> — Oxidation Is Loss, Reduction Is Gain (of electrons).</p>",1],["d-c10-oxidising-agent",10,"Oxidising agent<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A substance that oxidises another substance and is itself reduced.</p>","<p>e.g. oxygen, chlorine, acidified potassium manganate(VII).</p>",1],["d-c10-reducing-agent",10,"Reducing agent<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A substance that reduces another substance and is itself oxidised.</p>","<p>e.g. carbon, carbon monoxide, hydrogen, potassium iodide, reactive metals.</p>",1],["d-c11-acid",11,"Acid<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A substance whose aqueous solution contains H⁺ ions.</p><p>Acids are proton (H⁺) donors.</p>","<p>Acids have pH below 7 and turn litmus red. e.g. hydrochloric acid HCl, sulfuric acid H₂SO₄, nitric acid HNO₃, ethanoic acid CH₃COOH.</p>",1],["d-c11-neutralisation",11,"Neutralisation","<p>The reaction between an acid and a base (or alkali) to produce a salt and water.</p>","<p>Between an acid and an alkali the ionic equation is always: <strong>H⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq) → H₂O(l)</strong>.</p>",0],["d-c11-amphoteric-oxide",11,"Amphoteric oxide<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>An oxide that reacts with acids and with bases to produce a salt and water.</p>","<p><strong>Aluminium oxide (Al₂O₃) and zinc oxide (ZnO)</strong> are amphoteric.</p>",1],["d-c11-strong-acid",11,"Strong acid<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>An acid that is completely dissociated (ionised) in aqueous solution.</p><p>e.g. hydrochloric acid: HCl(aq) → H⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq)</p>","<p>Sulfuric and nitric acids are also strong acids.</p>",1],["d-c11-weak-acid",11,"Weak acid<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>An acid that is partially dissociated in aqueous solution.</p><p>e.g. ethanoic acid: CH₃COOH(aq) ⇌ H⁺(aq) + CH₃COO⁻(aq).</p>","<p>Most molecules stay un-ionised, so the H⁺ concentration is lower.</p>",1],["d-c11-base-and-alkali",11,"Base and alkali<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Bases are oxides or hydroxides of metals; alkalis are soluble bases.</p><p>Bases are proton (H⁺) acceptors.</p>","<p>Aqueous alkalis contain <strong>OH⁻ ions</strong> (pH above 7; litmus blue). e.g. bases CuO, MgO; alkalis NaOH, KOH, Ca(OH)₂, aqueous ammonia.</p>",1],["d-c12-hydrated-and-anhydrous-substan",12,"Hydrated and anhydrous substances","<p>A hydrated substance is a substance that is chemically combined with water; an anhydrous substance is a substance containing no water.</p>","<p>e.g. hydrated copper(II) sulfate CuSO₄·5H₂O (blue) → anhydrous CuSO₄ (white) on heating.</p>",0],["d-c12-water-of-crystallisation",12,"Water of crystallisation<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The water molecules present in hydrated crystals, including CuSO₄·5H₂O and CoCl₂·6H₂O.</p>","<p>(5 and 6 molecules of water per formula unit.)</p>",1],["d-c13-noble-gases-inert-gases",13,"Noble gases (inert gases)","<p>Group VIII elements (He, Ne, Ar…) — unreactive, monatomic gases. They are unreactive because their atoms have a full outer shell of electrons.</p>","<p>So they do not need to lose, gain or share electrons (they exist as single atoms).</p>",0],["d-c14-alloy",14,"Alloy","<p>A mixture of a metal with other elements.</p><p>Brass is a mixture of copper and zinc.</p><p>Stainless steel is a mixture of iron and other elements such as chromium, nickel and carbon.</p><p>Alloys can be harder and stronger than the pure metals and are more useful.</p>","<p>The other elements are usually metals, sometimes carbon.</p>",0],["d-c18-unsaturated-compound",18,"Unsaturated compound","<p>A compound with molecules in which one or more carbon–carbon bonds are not single bonds.</p>","<p>That is, it contains C=C. e.g. alkenes. An <strong>unsaturated hydrocarbon</strong> contains only C and H and has at least one C=C.</p>",0],["d-c18-homologous-series",18,"Homologous series<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A family of similar compounds with similar chemical properties due to the presence of the same functional group.</p>","<p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> <strong>General characteristics:</strong> (a) <strong>same functional group</strong>; (b) <strong>same general formula</strong>; (c) <strong>each member differs from the next by a –CH₂– unit</strong>; (d) <strong>a trend in physical properties</strong> (e.g. boiling point rises as the chain gets longer); (e) <strong>similar chemical properties</strong>.</p>",1],["d-c18-structural-isomers",18,"Structural isomers<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Compounds with the same molecular formula, but different structural formulae.</p>","<p>e.g. <strong>C₄H₁₀: CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃ (butane) and CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₃ (methylpropane)</strong>; <strong>C₄H₈: CH₃CH₂CH=CH₂ (but-1-ene) and CH₃CH=CHCH₃ (but-2-ene)</strong>.</p>",1],["d-c18-saturated-compound",18,"Saturated compound","<p>A compound with molecules in which all carbon–carbon bonds are single bonds.</p>","<p>e.g. alkanes.</p>",0],["d-c18-functional-group",18,"Functional group","<p>An atom or group of atoms that determine the chemical properties of a homologous series.</p>","<p>C=C (alkenes), –OH (alcohols), –COOH (carboxylic acids), –COO– (esters).</p>",0],["d-c18-hydrocarbon",18,"Hydrocarbon","<p>A compound that contains hydrogen and carbon only.</p>","<p>(Alkanes and alkenes.)</p>",0],["d-c18-structural-formula",18,"Structural formula<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>An unambiguous description of the way the atoms in a molecule are arranged.</p>","<p>Written without showing every bond, e.g. <strong>CH₂=CH₂, CH₃CH₂OH, CH₃COOCH₃</strong>.</p>",1],["d-c19-substitution-reaction",19,"Substitution reaction<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A reaction in which one atom or group of atoms is replaced by another atom or group of atoms.</p>","<p>e.g. an H atom in an alkane replaced by Cl.</p>",1],["d-c19-bonding-in-alkanes-and-alkenes",19,"Bonding in alkanes and alkenes","<p>W</p>","<p><strong>The bonding in alkanes is single covalent and alkanes are saturated hydrocarbons.</strong> <strong>The bonding in alkenes includes a double carbon–carbon covalent bond and alkenes are unsaturated hydrocarbons.</strong></p>",0],["d-c19-addition-reaction",19,"Addition reaction<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A reaction in which only one product is formed.</p>","<p>The reagent adds across the C=C double bond of an alkene (bromine, hydrogen, steam; also polymerisation).</p>",1],["d-c20-polymers",20,"Polymers","<p>Large molecules built up from many smaller molecules called monomers.</p>","",0],["d-c20-monomer",20,"Monomer","<p>One of the small molecules that join together to make a polymer.</p>","<p>e.g. ethene is the monomer of poly(ethene).</p>",0]]});
